  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nIR currently depicts mid level clouds pushing in across western\nand northern parts of the CWA from the southwest, extending from\nBaja Californian moisture extending through the Four Corner\nRegion. WV imagery shows the main finger of the PFJ extending\nacross southern Canada and a strong upper level low off the\ncentral Californian coastline. This low will begin its eastern\nextent Wednesday night into Thursday as it becomes a more open\nand progressive wave that will strengthen the southwest and\ndivergent flow into the CWA out of northern Utah. This is where\nthe stronger PVA anomalies will occur extending moisture content\ninto southern portions of the state by Thursday morning. \n\nThe aforementioned PFJ will start to dip across the Pacific\nnorthwest aligned with the main low as it becomes more open late\nThursday with an increasing jet max pushing 120-130kts. This\ndeepens the northern portions of this system with increased\ndivergence aloft. This increases the pressure gradient across\nmuch of the CWA about 5 to 7 mb across the state, strongest\nacross the south. Southwest winds push 40 to 55 mph Thursday\nafternoon during peak heating and mixing to the surface,\nstrongest across the wind corridor from Sweetwater through\neastern Fremont to Natrona Counties. Occasional gusts over 60 to\n65 mph possible (40-50%) for the higher terrain areas of South\nPass and outer drive of Casper Mountain. There is low confidence\non high wind criteria being met for this period of time, but\nwinds remain elevated overnight into Friday morning with wind \nshifts occur due to a passing cold front extending through the \nCWA into the afternoon hours Friday. \n\nPrecipitation wise, expect shower activity to increase out of\nnorthern Utah into southern portions of the CWA Thursday \nmorning pushing north throughout the day. Much of the QPF will \nfall west of the Divide with some higher terrain mountain snow \nexpected by late Thursday afternoon and especially overnight as \ntemperatures fall. This will be reinforced with FROPA on Friday,\nlowering freezing levels to 8-9kft as the system shifts east of\nthe Divide, and thus, impacting the Bighorns with mountain \nsnow. Mountain passes, to include Teton, Togwotee, and South\nPass, extending to Granite/Powder River, will see the impacts \nand hazards late Friday and into Saturday morning as the trough \nshifts northeast by day's end Saturday. Probabilities for \nsnowfall totals look to be of higher confidence across western \nmountains, to include the Winds, of 6 inches or more around \n60-70%. The Bighorns are of lower confidence around 30-40% for \nlater Friday and into the overnight hours. With snowfall totals \nof not great confidence, and being Friday for impacts, it has \nbeen decided to include surrounding offices to not issue any \nheadlines at this time. Model guidance has been up and down the \npast couple of days with this round bumping totals up once again\nand will need higher confidence to do so moving forward. \n\nBeyond that, expect more seasonable to just below average\ntemperatures post FROPA Friday and into the weekend to fell more\nFall-like for this time of year moving towards the later half of\nOctober. Another low extending out of the Gulf of Alaska looks\nto push down along the L/W pattern into the Pacific northwest\nlate Sunday that will near the CWA early next week. This will\nnot only reinforce this cooler air but continue the active\npattern that has a better track for stronger snow totals. Too\nsoon to judge the impacts at this point in time, but this looks\nto have a better track for higher mountain snow along and west\nof the Divide. Stay tuned for forecast updates and any headline\nissuance later Wednesday or Wednesday night as confidence grows\nfor impacts on Friday into Saturday.",
